course: dictionary
lesson: 15862
title: Unfounded
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: When something is unfounded, there is no proof for it. It is not based on facts or evidence, so it may not be true. «The rumour that the school would close was unfounded.» Nobody had any facts to back it up, so the rumour was not true.
explain: Look at the clues in the word. The start un- often means not, as in unhappy and untidy. Found means built on something solid, like a house on firm ground. So unfounded means not built on anything real.
explain: «Her fears about the dark were unfounded, because nothing was hiding under the bed.» The fears turned out to be wrong because there was no reason for them. To check your meaning, swap it into the sentence: "Her fears were not based on facts" still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Unfounded means not based on facts or evidence; not true.
fact: The prefix un- often means not, as in unhappy, untidy and unfair.
fact: Unfounded is often used with words like «rumours», «fears», «claims» and «accusations».
fact: «Completely unfounded» means there is no proof at all.
fact: An unfounded claim has no proof behind it, so careful people check the facts before they believe it.
exercise: «The rumour that sports day was cancelled was unfounded.» What does unfounded mean here? | Proven true by the teacher | *Not based on facts, so not true | Found under the bench | Very big and loud
exercise: Maya says her fears about the test were unfounded. What does she mean? | *Her worries had no good reason | Her worries were very big | She has proof the test will be hard | She is sure the test will be easy
exercise: Oliver says, «The claim is unfounded, so we should check the facts first.» Why should they check? | Because unfounded means very long | Because facts are boring | *Because a claim with no proof might not be true | Because the claim is definitely true
test: «The news report had no evidence, so its claims were completely unfounded.» What does completely unfounded mean? | Were printed in a newspaper | *Had no proof at all | Were shared by many people | Had lots of proof from experts
test: Which word has the same un- meaning as unfounded? | happy | *unhappy | found | fountain
exam: Ella says, «Her worries about the trip were unfounded, because the bus arrived on time.» What does Ella mean? | Her worries were very strong and true | *Her worries had no good reason, so they were wrong | The bus was late and the trip was cancelled | Ella has proof that the trip will be dangerous
